speechtext:
Madam Speaker, I regret to have to mention the matter of cost when dealing with the problem of senior citizens, especially-and I do emphasize this to the House-that of single people who receive the income supplement and many of whom are women. We do not usually say that they receive a sufficient number of special privileges which are not taken into account in the indexation and which are of course not deducted, such as senior citizen residences which clearly cost less. There remains one problem of which I have often talked with my colleagues in the House, and which would cost us $730 million to solve.
As for the other senior citizens, I refer to the couples, all of them are now over the poverty line, and this is why we often speak about the $35 a month, which makes $480 a year, to which quarterly indexation applies. The next indexation of the old age security pension and supplement is due in five weeks. It will amount to several dollars. It is worked out to take into account-and I regret that it cannot occur at the same moment as prices increase, but everyone knows that this is impossible. In five weeks, there will be an increase of several dollars in the amount of the pension and the supplement.
] English]
